Gates of this account
gettingstarted-06b-operating-system-access
Each row is a gate: something the administrator switches for this account. The page has six, Shell (sh), OS.EXECUTE (os), Remote files (net), the AI (ai), File transfer (xfer) and Scheduled jobs (jobs), and every one is off as delivered and reaches beyond SD. ON means this session holds it now; off means it does not. The row under the cursor is explained in plain words: what ON lets you do, what you can still do with it off, and the command that changes it. The page asks the system what the session may do; nothing is tried to find out.
Shell, OS.EXECUTE and Remote files are loaded when you sign on, so a change shows in the This session column at your next sign-on; the Register column is what the next sign-on will load, and only the administrator sees it. File transfer is read from the register each time receive or send is used, so both of its columns are what the register says now; it also needs XFER=1 in sd.conf. Scheduled jobs is read the same way, each time set.job or run.job is used: turned off, every job is refused at its next run. The AI's gate is the AI helper's own setting, read from its status for everyone and looked at at each request; the AI also needs a provider, a model and a key (tool.ai).
Only the administrator (type admin first) can change a gate. Enter turns the gate on the cursor's row ON or off through its own verb (set.os.access, set.net.access, set.ai gate, set.xfer.access, set.jobs.access), shown with its effect in a box answered Run or Cancel; Cancel is what Enter chooses. Turning a gate ON asks you to type its name. There is no key that turns everything on, and a session that is not the administrator cannot change anything from this page.
